- How does short-circuit evaluation affect pruning in a real solver?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests understanding of three-valued propagation on monotone constraints and the ability to design minimal backtracking strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic with ordering T > U > F
- Edge-wise monotone constraints on a directed graph
- Fixed-point propagation with backtracking for conflict resolution

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(nodes, edges, init) {
  const rank = { 'T': 2, 'U': 1, 'F': 0 };
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [from, to] of edges) {
      if (rank[nodes[to]] < rank[nodes[from]]) {
        nodes[to] = nodes[from];
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  }
  return nodes;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of temporal, multi-step constraints in a 3-valued logic setting. Requires constructing a concrete model and outlining a backtracking propagator that preserves non-F constraints while allowing Unknowns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued semantics for implication
- Temporal constraint propagation across time steps
- Unit/branching propagation in backtracking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: evaluateImp(A,B) returns Kleene(A,B)
function evaluateImp(A,B){ // A,B in {T,F,U}
  // ¬A ∨ B with Kleene ordering T>U>F
  const not = {T:'F', F:'T', U:'U'}[A];
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  // ⩒ is max by value order
  const values = [not, B];
  let best = 'F';
  for (const v of values) if (order[v] > order[best]) best = v;
  return best;
}
```
